00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.667
This is me
starting to get into it

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:35.534
and to get the feel for it.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:37.234
But you can all see this, right?

00:00:37.234 --> 00:00:39.534
So, Jimmy, I'm going to need
your help with this.

00:00:39.534 --> 00:00:41.000
-Yeah, wow.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.501
-Who was the first President of
the United States?

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:45.567
-George Washington.
-So, here he is. Okay.

00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:49.067
And he was the first one,
so that is one.

00:00:49.067 --> 00:00:51.300
Okay. So, you see there.
-Uh-huh.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.767
-So, he was the first president.
-Oh, yeah.

00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:54.701
-And after him came
a second president.

00:00:54.701 --> 00:00:56.100
So, we're going to do this
twice.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:57.434
-Ooh, I think --
-Second president.

00:00:57.434 --> 00:00:58.901
And so, when is your birthday?

00:00:58.901 --> 00:01:01.901
What the number of
your birthday?

00:01:01.901 --> 00:01:04.000
-Say it out loud?
-Yeah.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.767
-19.
-19. Okay.

00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:08.601
So, one. Okay?

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.100
And then a ninth of this, okay?

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.868
So --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:14.868
-1/9th.
-Okay.

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:16.868
[ Laughter ]
So, that's a ninth.

00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:19.434
-1/9th of the dollar.
-Okay. Right.

00:01:19.434 --> 00:01:21.934
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.934 --> 00:01:24.100
-So -- Alright.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.767
So, the third president -- So,
the third President isn't here.

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.734
Why? Because he's not here.

00:01:29.734 --> 00:01:30.667
-The third president
is not with us.

00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:32.100
-No? Okay. Nor is the fourth.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.200
So, we can see there isn't
a fourth president here.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.467
-Uh-huh.
-Okay.

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:37.434
And so, what is the month of
your birthday?

00:01:37.434 --> 00:01:41.200
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.300
What's the month?

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.100
-It is this. Nine.
-Nine. Okay.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.934
So, we had the ninth before,
so we've got this here.

00:01:46.934 --> 00:01:48.367
And then --
-Yep.

00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:50.100
-Now.
-Uh-oh.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.334
Nine.
-Okay. So, we got nine.

00:01:52.334 --> 00:01:54.133
So, if you count to nine --
You know how to count, right?

00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:55.501
You learn in school, right?

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:56.934
-Yeah, I did. I remember.

00:01:56.934 --> 00:01:58.701
-Do you? You remember?

00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:00.567
Have trouble counting?
-No.

00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:02.734
You want me to count to nine?
-Okay, so let's count slowly.

00:02:02.734 --> 00:02:04.133
So, one.
-One.

00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:06.634
-There you go. There's one.
-Yep.

00:02:06.634 --> 00:02:08.200
-Two.
-Two. Sorry.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.400
-Three.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:11.434
Four.

00:02:11.434 --> 00:02:13.801
Five. Six.

00:02:13.801 --> 00:02:16.567
Seven. Eight.

00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:19.968
-And then, so, who was the first
President of the United States?

00:02:19.968 --> 00:02:21.734
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.734 --> 00:02:23.300
-Don't even tell me.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.901
George Washington. Unbelievable.
Look at that.

00:02:25.901 --> 00:02:27.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.567
Wow. That's un--
It's unbelievable.

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:32.968
-Did it work?
-Wow. That's amazing.

00:02:32.968 --> 00:02:35.467
Yeah, it is. That was amazing.
That's really good.

00:02:35.467 --> 00:02:37.534
That's really --
-Can I watch that back?

00:02:37.534 --> 00:02:39.367
-No, no.
We don't have time for that.

00:02:39.367 --> 00:02:40.534
We don't have time for that.
-Okay.

00:02:40.534 --> 00:02:41.934
-But thank you so much
for bringing that trick.

00:02:41.934 --> 00:02:44.367
That's amazing.
You really are getting great.

00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:47.534
Let's talk about "Los Espookys."

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:48.968
-Yeah.
-Season two.

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:51.300
-Season two.
-Tomorrow on HBO, HBO Max.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.000
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.701
-I've been waiting so long
to see this.

00:02:54.701 --> 00:02:56.601
-Thanks.
-Gosh, I loved season one.

00:02:56.601 --> 00:02:59.968
It is right now 100% fresh on
Rotten Tomatoes, by the way,

00:02:59.968 --> 00:03:03.200
if you want to see something
100% fresh.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.200
If you haven't seen it,
can you catch everyone up?

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.901
What is the show about?

00:03:07.901 --> 00:03:12.100
-So, it's about a group of four
friends who have a business

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.968
where they hire themselves out
to scare people.

00:03:14.968 --> 00:03:16.534
It's sort of like the opposite
of "Scooby-Doo."

00:03:16.534 --> 00:03:18.834
They're like, "Just hire us
and we'll do, like,

00:03:18.834 --> 00:03:21.100
hauntings and seances and things
like that."

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.267
And it takes place in a sort of

00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:25.834
fictional Latin American
country.

00:03:25.834 --> 00:03:28.400
-Yeah, was that on purpose?
I'm assuming it was.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.334
Why would you -- why is it --
-Just because there's so many

00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:32.901
different accents from all
the different actors and stuff.

00:03:32.901 --> 00:03:36.033
We didn't want to have to do,
like, you know, to make it also,

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:38.701
like, exact that you have to
guess where they were from.

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:41.100
It's better to just keep it --
because we had some actors

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.501
from Mexico, Panama,
El Salvador.

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:47.234
-On the show before, you've done

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:49.601
many different impressions
and accents.

00:03:49.601 --> 00:03:51.801
You've done different states.

00:03:51.801 --> 00:03:53.534
I think you did all 50 states
at one point.

00:03:53.534 --> 00:03:55.801
Is there any way that --
Can you do some different

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:57.801
Spanish-speaking accents tonight
on our show

00:03:57.801 --> 00:03:58.834
and show everyone
the difference?

00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:01.133
-I can do some. Let's see.

00:04:01.133 --> 00:04:04.501
So, Mexico is
kind of sing-songy.

00:04:04.501 --> 00:04:06.934
And it's got a little bit of
drama to it.

00:04:06.934 --> 00:04:10.200
So it's a lot like --
[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.133
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.334 --> 00:04:25.534
Cuba is -- they sort of hover
around the consonants.

00:04:25.534 --> 00:04:27.701
[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:04:27.701 --> 00:04:29.367
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:41.934
Venezuela is kind of --
The tone of Venezuela is

00:04:41.934 --> 00:04:43.601
a little bit like, "Oh, you
shouldn't have done that."

00:04:43.601 --> 00:04:45.767
[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:04:45.767 --> 00:04:47.234
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:59.968 --> 00:05:02.167
And so, Colombia is
a little bit like the sort of

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:05.434
Ohio or the Oregon
of South America.

00:05:05.434 --> 00:05:08.167
It's almost like
a newscaster Spanish.

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:13.901
[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:15.501
Like leaning in on the S-H.

00:05:15.501 --> 00:05:19.100
Chile is a little bit like --
They're almost like saying,

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.801
"I don't feel like talking
right now."

00:05:20.801 --> 00:05:23.000
They're a little hesitant.
It's almost like Morse code.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.300
Like...
[ Speaking Spanish ]

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.133
[ Laughter ]

00:05:32.801 --> 00:05:34.734
That kind of thing.

00:05:34.734 --> 00:05:38.300
And then Brazil --
Brazil, that's Portuguese.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.667
It's not Spanish. But they're --

00:05:39.667 --> 00:05:42.234
[ Speaking foreign language ]
[ Laughter ]

00:05:42.234 --> 00:05:45.300
So, like, the feel of it is,
like, "Come to the party."

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.701
That's always been in there.
-Yes.

00:05:47.701 --> 00:05:53.033
"Come to the --"
-[ Speaking foreign language ]

00:05:53.033 --> 00:05:54.701
-That's how you do it
right there.

00:05:54.701 --> 00:05:57.334
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Aw, thanks. Thanks.

00:05:57.334 --> 00:05:59.667
That's close. Yeah, thanks.

